<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eli Whitney</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eli Whitney was an American inventor who patented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> gin in 1794, a machine that separated cotton fibres from their seeds far faster than by hand. It made short-staple cotton profitable across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a> and fuelled the expansion of cotton plantations and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; he also promoted interchangeable parts in the manufacture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eli-whitney.jpg" alt="Eli Whitney" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Finley Breese Morse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eli_Whitney_by_Samuel_Finley_Breese_Morse_1822.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
